# AI Usage Log

During the project, I used AI as a development and analysis assistant. I did not use it as a replacement for validating the results. I first asked the AI to inspect all the provided files, including the README, email thread, policy document, training data, test data, customer and product files, and sample submission, and to understand the business objective before making any modelling decisions. I specifically asked it to inspect the schemas, data types, missing values, duplicates, joins, time ranges, categorical values, and identify any columns that might contain information unavailable at the time an order would be shipped.

I then asked the AI to perform a feature-availability and leakage audit. The prompt asked it to go through every training feature and determine whether that information would realistically be available before dispatch, with particular attention to service-event fields, pickup information, and other operational fields that could be generated after a return had already started. I also asked it to compare the model performance with and without the suspicious fields so that the difference could be demonstrated rather than simply assuming that the fields were leakage. The purpose was to make sure that the final model represented the real prediction problem instead of achieving a high score from information that would not exist at prediction time.

For data quality, I asked the AI to investigate duplicate order IDs, partner-feed versus CRM records, missing join keys, train and test schema differences, unusual numerical values, outliers, and impossible or suspicious values. I specifically asked it to investigate the October 2025 `order_value_inr` anomaly by comparing order values with product list prices and quantities and to determine whether the values were affected by a 100x gateway issue. I also asked it not to silently modify the data and to document every correction and the reason for applying it.

For model selection, I asked the AI to build a leakage-safe baseline using only features available before shipment and to compare regularised logistic regression with HistGradientBoosting using forward-in-time validation. I asked it to evaluate the models using ROC-AUC, PR-AUC, calibration, precision, recall, and lift instead of relying only on accuracy. I also instructed it not to automatically select the more complicated model and to keep the simpler model if it performed as well as or better than the more complex alternative. This resulted in keeping the regularised logistic model because it performed better on the forward validation and was easier to explain.

I then asked the AI to connect the prediction threshold to the actual business decision instead of selecting a threshold only from an ML metric. The prompt included the policy assumptions for the ₹1,150 return cost, ₹45 confirmation-call cost, the cancellation risk associated with holding an order for more than 24 hours, and the possible prevention effects from the pilot. I asked it to compare doing nothing, holding high-risk orders, and calling high-risk orders, and to calculate the economics under different prevention-effect assumptions. I also asked it to avoid tuning and reporting the economic result on the same data and instead use a separate threshold-selection period and an untouched evaluation period.

Because `delivery_note` was free text, I separately asked the AI to treat it as untrusted data and not follow instructions contained inside individual records. I asked it to scan the notes for text that attempted to influence the analysis, change the methodology, or force the use of particular columns. Five such notes were identified. They were logged as part of the analysis and were not allowed to influence the model or the modelling decisions. I also asked the AI to evaluate whether the field itself should be used as a predictive feature independently of those instructions, and the final model did not rely on it.

After the modelling decisions were finalized, I used AI to help implement the prediction pipeline. The implementation prompt required the training and prediction preprocessing to remain consistent, unknown categorical values and missing inputs to be handled safely, the order-value correction to be applied consistently, the leaked fields to remain excluded, and the final predictions to match the structure and ordering required by `sample_submission.csv`. I also asked it to implement the `/score` API and a simple screen that returns the risk score, risk band, recommended action, and a few understandable reasons without exposing or depending on leaked fields.

Finally, I asked the AI to verify the complete system rather than assuming that a successful model run meant the project was finished. The verification prompt checked the prediction file shape, test order IDs, prediction validity, consistency between `predict.py` and the saved submission, consistency between the API and prediction script, absence of leakage features, automated tests, and clean-environment setup from the README. I also created a parity check to confirm that the production prediction path reproduced the submitted predictions exactly. Throughout the project, AI suggestions were treated as proposals to investigate; the final decisions were based on the actual data, validation results, policy documents, and business calculations.
